Impact:
Communication material (e.g. https://youtu.be/m247-ZdguLE);
Knowledge and relationships built among the participants during the workshop;
Some participants found the process to be personally transformative: the exercise had transformed the way she felt about and envisioned the future, and that it filled her with a sense of hope – but also a sense of responsibility, to encourage the initiatives and ideas that could help lead to this bright future.

This study defined NFF categories at each vertex and edge of the NFF triangle as Pareto optimal strategies between NFF indicators, highlighting the quantitative use of the NFF.

Furthermore, it was shown that pathways of possible management practices can change their value orientation over time within the NFF triangle.

&nbsp;

Description:
The workshop set out to open an inclusive and structured dialogue on the future of the Litoral Norte marine protected area (MPA) and to generate desirable (normative) visions capable of informing its integrated and adaptive management. Its specific goals were to:
- co-create plural, stakeholder-driven visions for the MPA over a 10-year horizon (to 2030);
- translate those visions into concrete, prioritised actions, each with identified responsible actors and timelines;
- map the MPA's key habitats, ecosystem services, threats and conflicts through participatory mapping;
- surface and reconcile the diverse perspectives of fishers, tourism operators, managers and scientists on MPA’s present and future.
Underlying these was a broader aim: to test a social-ecological approach to MPA governance that draws on both scientific and local knowledge, strengthening the legitimacy and social acceptance of conservation measures. Outputs were shared with the local and national conservation authorities to feed into the revision of the MPA's management plan.
